=== (II) THE CONVERSE LOOP SWAPS H±Pf  (verify on n=6, the n≡2 mod 4 case) ===
  enumerated all 32768 tournaments on n=6
  H(T*) = H(T) [converse-invariant, THM-203]:           True
  Pf(T*) = -Pf(T) [converse negates Pf at n≡2 mod 4]:    True
  ⟹ converse swaps the two outputs (H+Pf) ⟷ (H−Pf)
  H²−Pf² = 8Q with Q∈ℤ≥0 [THM-174]:                     True
  |Pf| distribution over all n=6 tournaments: {1: 15360, 3: 7680, 5: 4608, 7: 3840, 9: 1280}
  examples (H, Pf, H*, Pf*, Q):
     T:(H= 1,Pf=-1)  T*:(H= 1,Pf=+1)   outputs H±Pf = 0,2 -> swapped to 2,0;  product=8·0
     T:(H= 1,Pf=+1)  T*:(H= 1,Pf=-1)   outputs H±Pf = 2,0 -> swapped to 0,2;  product=8·0
     T:(H= 3,Pf=-3)  T*:(H= 3,Pf=+3)   outputs H±Pf = 0,6 -> swapped to 6,0;  product=8·0
  branch point (two outputs merge): Pf=0 ⟹ # tournaments with Pf=0: 0

=== contrast: n=4 (n≡0 mod 4) — the loop FIXES Pf, no swap ===
  Pf(T*) = +Pf(T) for all n=4 tournaments (n/2=2 even): True  ⟹ no output-swap at n≡0 mod 4

=== self-converse fixed points: the converse-iso parity constraint (n=6) ===
  among sampled tournaments, found 40 self-converse; for those with Pf≠0,
  the converse-iso P is an ODD permutation (det P = −1 = (−1)^3): True  (Pf=0 cases: 0)

=== (I) THE RANDOM/RADO TOURNAMENT: extension property converges, self-converse ===
  P(random tournament satisfies the k-extension axiom), k=1 then k=2:
    n= 3:  k=1 (no source/sink-pattern): 0.249   k=2: 0.000
    n= 4:  k=1 (no source/sink-pattern): 0.504   k=2: 0.000
    n= 5:  k=1 (no source/sink-pattern): 0.689   k=2: 0.000
    n= 6:  k=1 (no source/sink-pattern): 0.818   k=2: 0.000
    n= 7:  k=1 (no source/sink-pattern): 0.900   k=2: 0.000
    n= 8:  k=1 (no source/sink-pattern): 0.940   k=2: 0.000
    n= 9:  k=1 (no source/sink-pattern): 0.965   k=2: 0.000
    n=10:  k=1 (no source/sink-pattern): 0.984   k=2: 0.000
  ⟹ →1: the iid-arc random tournament a.s. satisfies ALL extension axioms
     = the Fraïssé limit = the GENERIC countable tournament (the Rado graph's odd twin).
  Self-converse: reversing iid-fair arcs is iid-fair ⟹ the generic tournament ≅ its
  converse — it is a FIXED POINT of the loop, the RANDOM pole opposite the cyclotomic
  worry-set pole (round tournaments on roots of unity, HYP-2089).
